layout: post
title: "Announcing email triggers for Appwrite Functions"
description: Appwrite Functions can now execute when an email arrives. Each function has a built-in email address, and you can connect your own domain with MX and TXT records.
date: 2026-10-28
cover: /images/blog/announcing-function-email-triggers/cover.avif
timeToRead: 6
author: torsten-dittmann
category: announcements
featured: false
callToAction: true
faqs:
  - question: "What is an email trigger in Appwrite Functions?"
    answer: "An email trigger executes an Appwrite Function when an email arrives at an address that is connected to the function. Appwrite receives the email, stores its content and attachments, and sends the parsed email to the function as JSON."
  - question: "Do I need to configure DNS to receive email in a function?"
    answer: "No. Each function has a built-in address, `<FUNCTION_ID>@<PROJECT_ID>.appwrite.email`, that works without DNS records once you turn on the email trigger. DNS records are necessary only when you connect your own domain. A custom domain needs an MX record and a TXT record."
  - question: "Can Appwrite Functions send email?"
    answer: "Email triggers only receive email. To send email, use [Appwrite Messaging](/docs/products/messaging) from the function, for example to reply to the sender."
  - question: "What happens when the same email arrives two times?"
    answer: "Your function can receive the same email more than one time. If Appwrite runs the function again for the same delivery, the `id` stays the same, so you can use it as the row ID. An email that the sender sends again gets a new `id`, so also compare `messageId` to catch those."
  - question: "How does a function get the attachments of an email?"
    answer: "The request body contains an `attachments` list. Each attachment has a name, a content type, a size, and a download URL. The URLs expire 15 minutes after Appwrite creates them, so download the files at the start of the function and upload them to Storage if you need them later."
---

Many apps receive work by email. Customers send support requests, vendors send invoices, and monitoring services send alerts. To process that email in code, you usually need an inbound email provider, a webhook endpoint, and a MIME parser.

Appwrite Functions now have **email triggers**. A function can execute when an email arrives at its address. Appwrite receives the email, parses it, stores the attachments, and sends the result to your function as JSON.

# How email triggers work

The email trigger is off by default, so you turn it on for each function that should receive email. After that, each email that arrives at an address of the function creates one asynchronous execution. The execution is a `POST` request, and the `x-appwrite-trigger` header is `email`.

Appwrite handles each email in this order:

1. Check each recipient address while the sending mail server connects. The address must belong to a function that has the email trigger on and an active deployment.
2. Receive the email over SMTP.
3. Parse the headers, the plain text body, the HTML body, and the attachments.
4. Store the original email, each attachment, and the parsed email.
5. Confirm receipt to the sending mail server.
6. Execute the function in the background with the parsed email as the request body.

The function receives JSON, so it needs no MIME parser and no mail server of its own.

# Receive email at the built-in address

{% info title="SMTP-DOCS-TODO: Screenshot pending (blog-built-in-address)" %}
